WEBVTT

00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:11,550
Hello everyone, this is Ann with Conor's Clinic and today we are going to talk a little bit about

00:00:12,430 --> 00:00:23,600
dairy and the MCM6 gene on your genetic report. So really the definition of dairy is any

00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:29,760
food or drink that contains milk. So I have some examples here. Most people know what what dairy is,

00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:42,400
but obviously anything that is cheese is dairy butter, ice cream, yogurt, whey protein, powdered milk,

00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:49,200
ghee is technically considered dairy because it is the the fat from butter, it's butter fat,

00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:58,880
condensed milk, sour cream. So all of these things are considered dairy and these are the components

00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:07,360
that make up dairy. So it's largely water and then we have the sugar component which is lactose,

00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:16,080
there's fats, there's different proteins so casing is a protein that dairy consists of and then

00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:24,320
it's vitamins and minerals. So many people can have an intolerance to lactose or an intolerance to

00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:31,520
some of the proteins in dairy. So casing and lactose are kind of the the offenders that people

00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:43,940
might have issues with when it comes to dairy. Oh, oops, excuse me, back to this one. So and then

00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:48,900
a lot of times people, you know, when we talk about, okay, how many sources of dairy are you

00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:53,940
consuming in a day and you kind of walk through your meals for the day? A lot of times people say,

00:01:53,940 --> 00:02:02,660
well, I eat eggs. So here's just a little reminder that eggs are not considered dairy. So dairy comes

00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:11,460
from cows or, you know, goats. So here's just a little funny. This is cheese, cheese comes from cows,

00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:19,380
this is an egg and egg comes from a chicken. So eggs do not equal dairy. So just a little reminder.

00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:23,140
So eggs when we're we're talking about dairy, we're not talking about eggs.

00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:32,420
And this is what the MCM6 gene looks like on your genetic report. So we've got two genes of interest

00:02:32,420 --> 00:02:38,260
here that are listed and obviously the variance column so you can either have a blank in that box

00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:45,940
or you can have a one or a two. This person has two. So double allele variance in both of these

00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:52,260
key genes for dairy. And really what this indicates, so this is the only gene that is kind of the

00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:56,740
opposite of what you think. So you see those variants there and you think, oh, I must be lactose

00:02:56,740 --> 00:03:02,420
intolerant because I have variants there. Actually, it's the opposite. And this is really the only gene where,

00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:13,220
you know, that is the case. We're having variants actually means that you are more likely to be able to

00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:19,540
digest the lactose component of dairy. Again, this is just looking at the lactose component. We're

00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:25,780
not looking at the proteins. So we're not looking at the casein in dairy. So you might have a sensitivity

00:03:25,780 --> 00:03:36,820
or allergy to the casein. So we're just looking at lactose. And really the MCM6 gene impacts another

00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:44,500
gene called LCT. And the LCT gene is the gene that provides instructions for making the enzyme

00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:51,780
lactase. So that's the enzyme that helps to break down the lactose, the sugar component and dairy.

00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:59,380
So without that enzyme, you can't really break that lactose down and you might have a lactose intolerance.

00:03:59,380 --> 00:04:09,380
And that is actually, as we age, the normal expression of this gene is for it to, you know,

00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:21,780
be on when we're young and we need dairy, we need that for growth. And so when we don't have variants

00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:29,060
here, the normal expression is for this gene to be turned on in childhood. And then slowly as we age,

00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:36,900
it kind of turns off and doesn't work anymore. Therefore, as you age, you might become sensitive to

00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:44,740
the lactose in dairy or lactose intolerant. That's the normal expression. When you have variants here,

00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:52,900
it actually means that these genes are likely still turned on into adulthood. Therefore, you might still

00:04:53,060 --> 00:05:01,060
be able to break down the lactose in dairy as an adult and you might not have any issues with lactose.

00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:09,460
So variants here are not a bad thing in this situation. Means the gene is likely turned on and you might

00:05:09,460 --> 00:05:15,860
not have any issues digesting lactose. So if you don't have variants, again, that's the normal

00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:22,740
expression. And normal expression is to kind of develop sensitivity or inability to break down lactose

00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:28,900
as we age. And you can kind of read through this and it goes into a little more detail.

00:05:30,340 --> 00:05:35,140
So this is how you're kind of interpreting your genetics. So again, we're just looking at that

00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:42,740
lactose component. So take that into consideration as you're kind of reflecting on yourself and

00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:51,540
your body's reaction to dairy when you consume it. And then if you do have a cancer diagnosis,

00:05:51,540 --> 00:06:00,020
so as I mentioned earlier, dairy is something that stimulates growth. And it's designed to do that

00:06:00,020 --> 00:06:09,140
because dairy was designed to take a baby and help them rapidly grow into a young adult and into

00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:14,340
adulthood. So there's growth factors that are in dairy that are supposed to be there for that case.

00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:22,020
When we're talking about a cancer diagnosis, growth is not something that we want to encourage.

00:06:22,020 --> 00:06:29,700
So a lot of times with our cancer patients, we are advising them or recommending they stay away

00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:35,780
from dairy. And it's for this reason. They might not, you might not have any issues processing dairy.

00:06:36,580 --> 00:06:44,020
Gut is fine. No issues with bowel movements or diarrhea, anything like that related to dairy.

00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:49,940
However, with cancer, we still want to limit dairy for this growth factor component.

00:06:51,300 --> 00:07:01,220
So take a look at your recommendations as far as the MCM16 and dairy. And hopefully this video helps

